\begin{proof}
Let $U$ be a neighborhood of $x$. Choose an open neighborhood $O$ of $x$ such that $O\subset U$. Since $X$ is locally compact at $x$, there exists a compact subspace $C$ containing a neighborhood $W$ of $x$. Replacing $W$ by an open neighborhood contained in it, assume $W$ is open. Set $A=C\setminus(O\cap W)$. Since $O\cap W$ is open in $C$, $A$ is compact. As $X$ is Hausdorff, there are disjoint open sets $V_1,V_2$ containing $x$ and $A$, respectively. Let $V=V_1\cap O\cap W$. Then $V$ is a neighborhood of $x$ and $V\subset C$. Because $C$ is compact in the Hausdorff space $X$, it is closed, so $\overline V\subset C$. Also, $V\subset V_1$ and $V_2$ is open and disjoint from $V_1$, hence $\overline V\cap V_2=\varnothing$. Therefore $\overline V\subset C\setminus A=O\cap W\subset O\subset U$. Finally, $\overline V$ is closed in the compact space $C$, so it is compact.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Let $U$ be a neighborhood of $x$. Choose an open set $O$ with $x\in O\subset U$. By local compactness at $x$, choose a compact neighborhood $C$ of $x$; equivalently, choose an open neighborhood $W$ of $x$ with $W\subset C$. The set $K=C\setminus(O\cap W)$ is closed in the compact space $C$, hence compact. Since $X$ is Hausdorff, the point $x$ and the compact set $K$ have disjoint open neighborhoods $V_1$ and $V_2$, respectively. Put $V=V_1\cap O\cap W$. Then $V$ is an open neighborhood of $x$ and $V\subset C$. The space $C$ is closed in $X$, because compact subsets of Hausdorff spaces are closed; consequently $\overline V\subset C$. Moreover, $V\subset V_1$ and $V_1\cap V_2=\varnothing$, with $V_2$ open, so $\overline V\cap V_2=\varnothing$. Thus $\overline V\subset C\setminus K=O\cap W\subset U$. Since $\overline V$ is closed in compact $C$, it is compact.
\end{proof}
